LEAD = {
    "headline": "QVC RUNS 60,000 HOURS OF LIVE TELEVISION A YEAR AND 200 HOURS A WEEK ON TIKTOK, AND IT SAYS THE SMALL ONE BROUGHT IN OVER A MILLION CUSTOMERS WHO HAD NEVER BOUGHT FROM IT AT ALL",
    "deck": "Modern Retail published a long interview with QVC's head of social commerce this morning.  The business built on live television selling now runs a second live operation at roughly a sixth of the airtime, and that is where the new customers came from.  The channel is not the interesting part.  The interesting part is that QVC rebuilt the format, built separate studios for it, and changed the number it coaches its presenters on.",
    "stamps": [
        ("QVC GROUP 10-Q · 4 AUG", "https://investors.qvcgrp.com/investors/sec-filings/all-sec-filings/content/0001254699-26-000018/qvc-20260630.htm"),
        ("MODERN RETAIL · 26 OCT", "https://www.modernretail.co/marketing/how-qvc-is-building-a-flywheel-across-tiktok-and-tv-ahead-of-the-holidays/"),
        ("PYMNTS · AUG", "https://www.pymnts.com/news/retail/2026/qvc-group-emerges-from-bankruptcy-with-600-million-dollar-asset-backed-facility/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Krystyna Taheri runs social commerce at QVC Group, and Modern Retail published a question-and-answer with her this morning.  Her two volume figures, in her own words: we still do about <mark>60,000 hours a year of live TV</mark>.  And then, on TikTok, we do about <mark>200 hours a week of just live</mark>.  Divide the television number by 52 and QVC is putting out roughly 1,150 hours of live television a week, which makes the TikTok operation about a sixth of the airtime.  That division is this brief's, not hers.",
        "Now what the small one did.  Taheri: I will say, we generated <mark>over 1 million net new customers on TikTok last year</mark>, which was massive.  And then the line that matters more than the number: that means <mark>they've never shopped on QVC, whether it be on TV or dot-com</mark>.  Not switchers.  Not the television audience moving channel.  People the company had never sold anything to.",
        "Hold that against the rest of the business before you get excited.  QVC Group filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection on <mark>16 April 2026</mark> and came out on <mark>6 August</mark> having cut debt by more than <mark>5 billion dollars</mark>.  Its own quarterly filing shows net revenue of <mark>1,765 million dollars for the three months to 30 June, against 1,984 million a year earlier</mark>.  The television business is shrinking.  The 200 hours a week is where the growth is.",
        "Here is the part worth copying, and it is three separate decisions rather than one.  First, the format is not the television format made shorter.  Taheri: it's a lot faster.  You're talking <mark>20-second, 30-second hooks and sells, versus a seven- to eight-minute segment</mark>, which is what we do on TV.  Second, the production is not the television production.  QVC built <mark>10 live studios specifically for TikTok</mark>, filmed on a phone: we have a full robotics team for our TV studios that do the cameras, we don't for TikTok.  The sets are different.  The cuts are different.  Each one is built for one department — fashion that looks like you are standing in a closet, beauty shot close over a tray, home with a fireplace behind it, and a kitchen set they are working towards cooking live on.",
        "Third, and this is the one nobody copies, they changed what the presenters are graded on.  Hosts get weekly tape reviews with a producer, and the training is about which number to watch while you are live.  Taheri: <mark>how do they think about the click-through rate?  How do they think about the click-to-order rate?  Obviously, the revenue is an output of those metrics</mark>.  So we try to teach them not to focus just on the revenue that's coming in, but instead focus on those engagement metrics, because that will lead to conversions.  Read that as a craft instruction, because that is what it is.  A presenter watching revenue slows down and keeps selling the item in front of them.  A presenter watching click-to-order keeps the pace that gets the next item clicked.",
        "And now the hole in it, which is large.  There is <mark>no revenue figure, no sales figure, no conversion rate and no average order value anywhere in the interview</mark>.  QVC says it started streaming 24 hours a day on TikTok in April 2025, that it works with hundreds of thousands of creators, that some livestreams run eight to ten hours, and that it has a three-day event on 6 to 8 November with Martha Stewart, John Legend and Teri Hatcher.  What it will not say is what any of it earns.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("200 hrs", "live selling a week on TikTok, against roughly 1,150 hours a week on television — this brief's division of QVC's own 60,000 hours a year"),
        ("1M+", "net new customers QVC says it got on TikTok last year, who had never shopped it on television or on its website"),
        ("10", "live studios built specifically for TikTok, filmed on a phone, with no robotics team on the cameras"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "Every performance figure here is QVC's own count and none of it is independently measured.  The article contradicts itself on the customer number: the editorial introduction says nearly 1 million new United States customers on TikTok Shop in 2025, while Taheri's quote says over 1 million net new customers on TikTok last year.  Those cannot both be right, the scope differs between them — one names QVC plus its sibling HSN on TikTok Shop in the United States, the other says only on TikTok — and this item uses her quoted wording with that caveat attached.  The roughly 1,150 hours a week figure is this brief's arithmetic on the stated 60,000 a year, not a published number.  The 10 studios are confirmed but only five are named in the piece.  No revenue, sales, conversion or order-value figure appears anywhere, and Taheri explicitly deflects revenue.  The bracketed insertions in her quote about filming on a phone are Modern Retail's, not her words.  QVC's plan confirmation date is given as 15 July by one outside summary and as a confirmation order entered on 20 July in the company's own filing; the filing is the one to use.  The debt reduction is quoted at more than 5 billion dollars because that is QVC's own wording, and other framings of the same restructuring run to 5.3 billion.",
    "so_what": "Almost every owned-video conversation treats the problem as reach or budget.  QVC has reach and found that reach was not the variable.  What it changed was the shape of the thing, where it was made, and which number the person on camera is watching while the camera is on — and the result was a million people it had never sold to.  The third of those is the one nobody does, because the metric a presenter watches is treated as a talent note rather than as a lever on revenue.",
    "do_this": "Pick the one live or long-form format you already run and rebuild it for a second platform from scratch this week rather than cutting it down: write the first twenty seconds as a complete hook and sell, and shoot it on a phone in a set dressed for that one product category.  Then change what you brief the on-camera talent to watch.  Give them the click-through and click-to-order figures live and tell them explicitly not to chase revenue, and compare that session against your usual one on orders per hour.  And when a client quotes a live-commerce customer number at you, ask for revenue per new customer and the repeat rate before you plan anything on it, because QVC published neither.",
}

